  <munus fontes="ce us0101">Mother of God</munus>
  <munus fontes="mr0908 mr1208">virgin</munus>
  <patrocinium fontes="us1208">United States of America (as the Immaculate Conception)</patrocinium>
  <patrocinium fontes="mrpatr">aeronauts (as Our Lady of Loreto)</patrocinium>
  <descriptio>
    <paragraphus fontes="ce">The Blessed Virgin Mary is the mother of Jesus Christ, the Mother of God. The Gospels say surprisingly little about her. St. Luke places her at Nazareth in Galilee at the Annunciation, a virgin espoused to Joseph, and the Catholic Encyclopedia reads his words as direct testimony to her descent from David. The names of her parents, Joachim and Anna, come from a tradition based on the Protoevangelium of James, an apocryphal gospel of the late second century. With Joseph she went from Nazareth to Bethlehem for the enrolment ordered by Caesar Augustus, and she stood by the cross of her Son. When Nestorius denied her the title Mother of God, the Council of Ephesus proclaimed her Theotokos in the true sense of the word.</paragraphus>
    <paragraphus fontes="ce mr1208">Where she lived after Pentecost is not certain. By the early fifth century there was a fairly general tradition that she died in Jerusalem and was buried in Gethsemani, which rests on a firmer basis than the report of her death at Ephesus; there is no certain tradition of the year. The feast of her Assumption is most probably the oldest of her feasts. The Martyrology records that on 8 December Pius IX solemnly declared that she had been preserved by a special privilege of God from the stain of original sin.</paragraphus>
  </descriptio>
  <fons id="ce" opus="ce1913" locus="Bl. Virgin Mary" url="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Catholic_Encyclopedia_(1913)/Bl._Virgin_Mary">
    <excerptum>The Blessed Virgin Mary is the mother of Jesus Christ, the mother of God.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>The reader of the Gospels is at first surprised to find so little about Mary</excerptum>
    <excerptum>According to Luke 1:26, Mary lived in Nazareth, a city in Galilee, at the time of the Annunciation.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>to a virgin espoused to a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David" (Luke 1:26-27); the last clause "of the house of David" does not refer to Joseph, but to the virgin</excerptum>
    <excerptum>thus we have a direct inspired testimony to Mary's Davidic descent.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>a tradition founded upon the report of the Protoevangelium of James, an apocryphal Gospel which dates from the end of the second century. According to this document the parents of Mary are Joachim and Anna.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>explains how Joseph and Mary journeyed from Nazareth to Bethlehem in obedience to a decree of Caesar Augustus which prescribed a general enrolment.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>While Jesus was hanging on the cross, "there stood by the cross of Jesus, his mother, and his mother's sister, Mary Cleophas, and Mary Magdalen.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>The contention of Nestorius denying to Mary the title "Mother of God" [75] was followed by the teaching of the Council of Ephesus proclaiming Mary to be Theotokos in the true sense of the word.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>It has been seen that we have no absolute certainty as to the place in which Mary lived after the day of Pentecost.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>we may conclude that about the beginning of the fifth century there existed a fairly general tradition that Mary had died in Jerusalem, and had been buried in Gethsemani. This tradition appears to rest on a more solid basis than the report that Our Lady died and was buried in or near Ephesus.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>There is no certain tradition as to the year of Mary's death.</excerptum>
    <excerptum>The feast of the Assumption is most probably the oldest among all the feasts of Mary properly so called.</excerptum>
  </fons>
